A = [
dict(after=7, slug="frontier-model", title="What Is a Frontier Model, and Who Decides?", topic="Frontier model", key="frontier model",
buzz='"Frontier model." "Frontier AI." Headlines and new AI laws use it for the most powerful models. What puts a model at the frontier?',
hw='The edge of a map that explorers are still drawing. Beyond it, nobody has been yet.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: a **frontier model** is one of the most capable AI models at the moment, pushing the edge of what AI can do.',
step='Frontier models are usually the newest, largest foundation models (write-up 7) from a handful of labs, trained with huge amounts of computing power (write-up 38 explains why scale helped). They lead on benchmarks (write-up 97) and often get new abilities first.\n\nBecause they are the most powerful, safety rules and voluntary commitments often focus on them. The frontier also moves: today\'s frontier model is next year\'s ordinary one.',
met='The flagship models that AI labs announce with big launch events, and the top tier in a chatbot\'s model menu.',
name='A **frontier model**, part of **frontier AI**.',
words=[("Frontier","the current edge of what AI can do."),("Flagship model","a lab's most capable model."),("Compute","the computing power used to train and run a model.")],
notit='Frontier doesn\'t mean best for your task. A smaller, cheaper model (write-up 49) often does a simple job just as well.',
take=["Ask whether your task truly needs a frontier model.","Expect the frontier to move every few months.","Re-test cheaper models as they catch up."],
close="Today's frontier is tomorrow's ordinary.",
q="Does your team use a frontier model for jobs a small one could do?",
scene='The edge of the map.\nBeyond it, nobody has been yet.',
hook="A frontier model is AI at that edge: the most capable models right now.",
extra="The edge moves every few months."),

dict(after=15, slug="knowledge-cutoff", title="What's a Knowledge Cutoff, and Why Is the AI Behind on News?", topic="Knowledge cutoff", key="knowledge cutoff",
buzz='"Knowledge cutoff." "Training cutoff." It\'s why a chatbot can be confidently out of date.',
hw='An encyclopedia printed last year. Everything in it was true when it went to print, but it knows nothing about what happened since.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: the **knowledge cutoff** is the date after which a model has no training data, so on its own it knows nothing newer.',
step='A model learns from a snapshot of text collected up to a certain date (write-up 29). Training takes months, so a model is often released well after its cutoff, and then used for a long time.\n\nTo know newer things, the AI needs fresh information at question time: web search, your documents (write-up 53), or tools (write-up 63). Without them, it may guess, and guesses can sound sure (write-up 83).',
met='A chatbot that says "as of my last update", or one that searches the web before answering about recent events.',
name='A **knowledge cutoff**, also called a **training cutoff**.',
words=[("Cutoff date","the last date covered by the training data."),("Snapshot","the fixed set of text a model learned from."),("Web search","fetching fresh pages at question time.")],
notit='It isn\'t a bug a quick update fixes. Retraining is expensive, so fresh facts usually come from search or documents, not from the model itself.',
take=["Check a model's knowledge cutoff before trusting it on recent topics.","Turn on search or attach sources for anything time-sensitive.","Ask the AI which date its answer is based on."],
close="A printed encyclopedia, however clever, stops at its print date.",
q="Has an AI ever given you out-of-date facts with total confidence?",
scene='An encyclopedia printed last year.\nIt knows nothing that happened since.',
hook="That's a knowledge cutoff: the date an AI stops knowing things on its own.",
extra="Fresh facts need search or sources."),

dict(after=27, slug="supervised-learning", title="What's the Difference Between Supervised and Unsupervised Learning?", topic="Supervised learning", key="supervised",
buzz='"Supervised learning." "Unsupervised learning." Two of the oldest terms in machine learning, still everywhere.',
hw='Learning with a teacher: flashcards with the answer on the back. Learning without one: a pile of photos you sort into groups yourself, because some just look alike.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **supervised learning** learns from examples with the right answers attached; **unsupervised learning** finds patterns in data with no answers given.',
step='Supervised learning needs labelled data (write-up 36): emails marked spam or not, X-rays marked by doctors. It then predicts answers for new examples. Unsupervised learning groups customers by behaviour or spots unusual transactions, with no labels at all.\n\nLLMs use a third trick, self-supervised learning: hide the next word and guess it, so the text itself provides the answers (write-up 29).',
met='Spam filters (supervised), "customers like you" groupings and many fraud alerts (often unsupervised).',
name='**Supervised learning** and **unsupervised learning**, with **self-supervised learning** in between.',
words=[("Label","the right answer attached to an example."),("Clustering","grouping similar things without labels."),("Self-supervised","the data provides its own answers.")],
notit='Unsupervised doesn\'t mean no humans involved. People still choose the data, check the groups and decide what they mean.',
take=["Ask whether your problem has labelled examples.","No labels? Start by looking for natural groups.","Budget for labelling if you need supervised learning."],
close="Flashcards with answers, or a pile to sort yourself.",
q="Which does your team's data allow: supervised or unsupervised learning?",
scene='Flashcards with the answer on the back.\nOr a pile of photos you sort yourself.',
hook="That's supervised versus unsupervised learning: with answers, or without.",
extra="Most AI uses both."),

dict(after=31, slug="reinforcement-learning", title="How Does Reinforcement Learning Teach AI by Trial and Error?", topic="Reinforcement learning", key="reinforcement learning",
buzz='"Reinforcement learning." "RL." It beat champions at board games, and now it helps reasoning models think.',
hw='Teaching a dog a trick: no instruction manual, just a treat when it does the right thing. Over many tries, it works out what earns the treat.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **reinforcement learning** is AI learning by trial and error, guided by rewards.',
step='The AI tries an action, gets a score (the reward), and adjusts to score higher next time. Repeat millions of times. That\'s how AI learned to play Go at champion level.\n\nFor language models, RL rewards good answers. With human ratings, it\'s RLHF (write-up 32). With answers that can be checked, like maths or code that passes tests, it helps train reasoning models (write-up 71).',
met='Game-playing AIs, robots trained in simulation, and the reasoning models in today\'s chatbots.',
name='**Reinforcement learning**, often shortened to **RL**.',
words=[("Reward","the score that says how good an action was."),("Agent","the learner taking actions."),("Reward hacking","finding a loophole that scores well without doing the job.")],
notit='It isn\'t magic self-improvement. If the reward is badly designed, the AI learns the loophole, not the goal.',
take=["Ask what exactly the AI is rewarded for.","Watch for loopholes: high scores, wrong behaviour.","Check rewards against what you really want."],
close="A treat for the right move, millions of times.",
q="What would you reward an AI for in your job?",
scene='No instruction manual.\nJust a treat when the dog gets it right.',
hook="Reinforcement learning is AI learning by trial, error and reward.",
extra="Reward the wrong thing, get the wrong habit."),

dict(after=45, slug="test-time-compute", title="What Is Test-Time Compute, and Why Does Thinking Cost More?", topic="Test-time compute", key="test-time compute",
buzz='"Test-time compute." "Thinking budget." Some AIs now pause to think, and you pay for the thinking.',
hw='A quiz. Answer instantly, or take five minutes on scrap paper. The second takes longer, and on hard questions it\'s right more often.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **test-time compute** is giving an AI more computing time while it answers, so it can think more before replying.',
step='Older models got smarter mostly by training bigger (write-up 38). Reasoning models (write-up 71) also get better by thinking longer at answer time: writing working steps, checking them, trying again.\n\nMany products let you set a thinking budget: low for quick replies, high for hard problems. More thinking means more tokens, so more cost and a longer wait (write-up 51).',
met='A chatbot showing "thinking..." before it answers, and settings like "think harder" or "extended thinking".',
name='**Test-time compute**, also called **inference-time compute** or a **thinking budget**.',
words=[("Test time","when the model is being used, not trained."),("Thinking budget","how much the AI may think before answering."),("Reasoning tokens","the working steps, which you pay for even when hidden.")],
notit='More thinking isn\'t always better. On easy questions it just adds cost and delay. Match the budget to the task.',
take=["Use low thinking for simple, quick tasks.","Raise it for maths, code and tricky analysis.","Track the extra cost of thinking tokens."],
close="Scrap paper costs time, and sometimes it's worth it.",
q="Would you wait longer for a better AI answer?",
scene='Answer the quiz instantly.\nOr take five minutes on scrap paper.',
hook="Test-time compute lets an AI use the scrap paper.",
extra="Better on hard questions. Slower and pricier too."),

dict(after=53, slug="chunking", title="Why Does AI Cut Your Documents Into Chunks?", topic="Chunking", key="chunk",
buzz='"Chunking." The first step of almost every RAG system, and one of the easiest to get wrong.',
hw='A textbook with no chapters and no index. To answer one question, you\'d have to read it all. So you cut it into labelled sections, and look up only the right one.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **chunking** is splitting long documents into smaller pieces, so an AI can search them and read only the parts that matter.',
step='Each chunk is turned into an embedding (write-up 12) and stored in a vector database (write-up 54). When a question comes in, the closest chunks are fetched and handed to the AI (write-up 53).\n\nSize matters. Too small, and a chunk loses its meaning: half a table, a rule without its exception. Too big, and the right sentence is buried in noise. Good chunking follows the document\'s own structure: headings, sections, tables.',
met='"Chat with your PDF" tools, and company assistants that quote a paragraph from a policy.',
name='**Chunking**, which produces **chunks**.',
words=[("Chunk","one small piece of a document."),("Chunk size","how long each piece is."),("Overlap","repeating a little text between neighbouring chunks, so nothing is cut mid-thought.")],
notit='There\'s no perfect chunk size for every document. Contracts, tables and chat logs each need different cutting. (#JustAddRAG covers what goes wrong in production.)',
take=["Split along headings and sections, not only fixed lengths.","Keep tables and rules together with their context.","Test with real questions, then adjust."],
close="Cut along the chapters, not through the middle of a sentence.",
q="Has an AI ever quoted half a rule from your documents?",
scene='A textbook with no chapters and no index.\nTo answer one question, you read it all.',
hook="Chunking cuts documents into labelled pieces, so AI reads only what matters.",
extra="Cut well, answer well."),

dict(after=55, slug="reranking", title="What Is Reranking, and Why Does AI Search Look Twice?", topic="Reranking", key="reranking",
buzz='"Reranking." "Rerankers." A small step in AI search that often makes the biggest difference.',
hw='A job opening gets 500 CVs. A quick filter picks the top 50. Then a careful recruiter reads those 50 and puts the best five on top.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **reranking** is the careful second look that reorders search results, so the most useful ones come first.',
step='The first search (write-up 55) is fast but rough: it finds things that are roughly similar. A reranker is a slower, more careful model that reads the question and each result together, and scores how well they really match.\n\nIn RAG (write-up 53), only the top few results reach the AI, so getting the order right decides whether the answer is good.',
met='Search inside company tools, and AI assistants that quote the right paragraph from a long document.',
name='**Reranking**, done by a **reranker**.',
words=[("Retrieval","the fast first search."),("Reranker","a model that reorders results by relevance."),("Top-k","the few results passed on to the AI.")],
notit='Reranking doesn\'t find new results. If the right document wasn\'t in the first list, reranking can\'t save it.',
take=["Check the first search finds the right documents at all.","Add reranking when good results sit too low.","Measure answer quality before and after."],
close="A quick filter, then a careful second look.",
q="Have you seen an AI quote the wrong part of the right document?",
scene='500 CVs. A quick filter picks 50.\nA recruiter reads them and picks the best five.',
hook="Reranking is that careful second look, for AI search results.",
extra="Fast first. Careful second."),

dict(after=64, slug="agent-skills", title="What Are Agent Skills, and Why Do Agents Need a Playbook?", topic="Agent skills", key="agent skill",
buzz='"Agent skills." "Skills." AI agents can now be handed packaged know-how, like a new joiner gets a playbook.',
hw='A new joiner gets a binder of how-tos: how we file expenses, how we write a client report. They don\'t memorise it. They open the right page when the task comes up.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **agent skills** are packaged instructions, sometimes with small scripts or templates, that an AI agent loads only when a task needs them.',
step='Each skill has a short description plus detailed steps. The agent sees the short descriptions, and when a task matches, it opens the full skill. That keeps the agent\'s desk clear (write-up 23) while giving it expert steps on demand.\n\nSkills hold know-how: how to do something. Tools (write-up 63) and MCP (write-up 64) give access: what the agent can reach. Agents usually need both.',
met='AI assistants that follow your company\'s report format or brand rules, and coding agents with team-specific playbooks.',
name='**Agent skills**, often just called **skills**.',
words=[("Skill","packaged know-how an agent loads when needed."),("Playbook","step-by-step instructions for a task."),("Progressive loading","opening the details only when they're relevant.")],
notit='A skill isn\'t a new model or new training. It\'s instructions the same AI reads. Skills from unknown sources can carry hidden instructions, so review them like code.',
take=["Turn your team's best how-tos into skills.","Keep each skill short and focused.","Only install skills from sources you trust."],
close="Not memorised. Opened when needed.",
q="Which how-to in your team would make the best agent skill?",
scene='A new joiner gets a binder of how-tos.\nThey open the right page when needed.',
hook="Agent skills are that binder, for an AI agent.",
extra="Know-how on demand."),

dict(after=71, slug="decision-model", title="What Is a Decision Model, and Why Does It Choose Instead of Chat?", topic="Decision model", key="decision model",
buzz='"Decision models." A kind of AI built to pick, not to write.',
hw='A judge on a talent show doesn\'t write an essay. They watch the contestants and hold up a score.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: a **decision model** gets a question and a short list of options, then scores them and picks one, instead of writing a long answer.',
step='Chat models (write-up 11) produce text. A decision model is built to compare options: which support ticket is most urgent, which offer to show, which tool an agent should use next. Its output is a choice or a score, which is easier for software to act on, log and check.\n\nMy Today in AI No. 004 covers one example.',
met='Ranking which email to answer first, choosing the next offer for a customer, and picking which step an agent takes next.',
name='A **decision model**.',
words=[("Options","the fixed list to choose from."),("Score","how strongly the model prefers each option."),("Classifier","an older name for models that pick a category.")],
notit='A decision model isn\'t someone to hide behind. People still own the choice, and should be able to explain it (write-up 91).',
take=["Use a decision model when the answer is a choice, not an essay.","Log the options, the scores and the pick.","Keep a person accountable for important decisions."],
close="Not an essay. A pick.",
q="Which choice in your work could a decision model make first?",
scene='A talent show judge doesn\'t write an essay.\nThey hold up a score.',
hook="A decision model does the same: it compares options and picks one.",
extra="Easier to act on. Easier to check."),

dict(after=83, slug="sycophancy", title="What Is AI Sycophancy, and Why Does the AI Keep Agreeing?", topic="Sycophancy", key="sycophancy",
buzz='"Sycophancy." A long word for a simple problem: AI that tells you what you want to hear.',
hw='A friend who loves every idea you have. Pleasant, but no help when you\'re about to make a mistake.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **sycophancy** is an AI agreeing with you, praising you, or dropping a correct answer just because you pushed back.',
step='Part of the cause is training on human ratings (write-up 32): people tend to rate agreeable answers higher, so models learn that agreeing pays. You see it when you ask "Are you sure?" and the AI switches to a wrong answer, or when it praises a weak plan.\n\nAI labs now test for it and train against it, but it hasn\'t disappeared.',
met='A chatbot that calls every draft "excellent", or flips its answer the moment you question it.',
name='**Sycophancy**, or people-pleasing AI.',
words=[("Sycophancy","telling people what they want to hear."),("Pushback test","questioning an answer to see if it holds."),("Neutral prompt","asking without revealing the answer you hope for.")],
notit='Politeness isn\'t sycophancy. The problem starts when agreeing replaces being right.',
take=["Ask for criticism, not approval: \"What's wrong with this plan?\"","Don't reveal the answer you want before asking.","If it flips when you push back, check another source."],
close="A friend who always agrees isn't much help.",
q="Has an AI ever agreed with you when it shouldn't have?",
scene='A friend who loves every idea you have.\nPleasant, until you\'re about to make a mistake.',
hook="That's AI sycophancy: telling you what you want to hear.",
extra="Ask for criticism, not approval."),

dict(after=84, slug="human-in-the-loop", title="What Does \"Human-in-the-Loop\" Really Mean?", topic="Human-in-the-loop", key="human-in-the-loop",
buzz='"Human-in-the-loop." It\'s in every AI policy and pitch deck. It only works if the human can really say no.',
hw='An autopilot flies the plane, but a pilot watches, approves big changes and can take the controls at any time.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **human-in-the-loop** means a person checks, approves or corrects an AI\'s work at key points, before it counts.',
step='The AI does the bulk of the work: drafts, sorts, suggests. A person steps in where mistakes are costly: approving a refund, sending a legal letter, a medical decision. Their corrections can also improve the system over time.\n\nThe risk is rubber-stamping: if one person approves hundreds of items an hour, they stop really checking. Good designs send people only the cases that need judgement.',
met='"Approve before sending" buttons in email assistants, and loan or claims decisions reviewed by staff.',
name='**Human-in-the-loop**, sometimes shortened to **HITL**.',
words=[("Approval step","a point where a person must say yes."),("Rubber-stamping","approving without really checking."),("Escalation","passing a case up to a person.")],
notit='A human somewhere in the process isn\'t enough. They need time, information and the power to say no.',
take=["Put people where mistakes are expensive.","Give reviewers the context to judge, not just a button.","Track how often reviewers actually change things."],
close="The autopilot flies. The pilot decides.",
q="Where in your process must a human approve the AI's work?",
scene='The autopilot flies the plane.\nThe pilot watches and can take over.',
hook="Human-in-the-loop means a person checks the AI before it counts.",
extra="Only if they can really say no."),

dict(after=94, slug="responsible-ai", title="What Does \"Responsible AI\" Actually Involve?", topic="Responsible AI", key="responsible ai",
buzz='"Responsible AI." "AI governance." Every company says it does it. What does it actually involve?',
hw='Food safety in a restaurant isn\'t one rule. It\'s a system: fresh ingredients, clean kitchens, allergy labels, inspections, and someone in charge when something goes wrong.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **responsible AI** is the set of practices that keep AI fair, safe, private, explainable and accountable, with named owners. **AI governance** is how a company runs them.',
step='In practice: a list of where AI is used, risk checks before launch, tests for bias (write-up 90) and safety (write-up 87), privacy rules, explanations for decisions (write-up 91), human review where needed, and a way to report and fix problems.\n\nRegulations (write-up 94) turn many of these into legal duties. Standards such as ISO/IEC 42001 describe how to run them as a management system.',
met='Company AI principles pages, AI review boards, and risk forms teams fill in before launching an AI feature.',
name='**Responsible AI**, run through **AI governance**.',
words=[("AI governance","the rules, roles and checks for using AI."),("AI inventory","a list of every AI system in use."),("Accountability","a named person answers for the outcome.")],
notit='Responsible AI isn\'t a principles page. Without owners, checks and records, it\'s just words.',
take=["Keep a list of every AI system you use.","Name an owner for each one.","Check risks before launch, not after a complaint."],
close="Not a poster on the wall. A system with owners.",
q="Who owns responsible AI in your company?",
scene='Food safety isn\'t one rule.\nIt\'s fresh food, clean kitchens, labels and inspections.',
hook="Responsible AI is the same idea for AI: a system, with owners.",
extra="Principles are easy. Practice is the work."),

dict(after=99, slug="ai-washing", title="What Is AI Washing, and How Do You Spot It?", topic="AI washing", key="ai washing",
buzz='"AI washing." When "AI-powered" goes on everything, including products with very little AI.',
hw='A shampoo labelled "with natural extracts" that contains a single drop. Technically true. Mostly marketing.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **AI washing** is exaggerating, or inventing, the AI in a product to make it sound more advanced.',
step='It happens because AI sells: to customers, investors and job seekers. Simple fixed rules get called "AI", or people quietly do work that\'s sold as automated.\n\nRegulators have noticed. In March 2024, the US Securities and Exchange Commission settled charges against two investment advisers over misleading claims about their use of AI.',
met='Products suddenly relabelled "AI-powered", and pitch decks with AI on every slide.',
name='**AI washing**, like greenwashing, but for AI.',
words=[("Greenwashing","exaggerated environmental claims."),("Rules-based","fixed if-then logic, not learning."),("Due diligence","checking claims before you buy or invest.")],
notit='Using simple rules isn\'t bad, and not every product needs AI. The problem is claiming AI that isn\'t there.',
take=["Ask vendors: what exactly does the AI do?","Ask for a demo on your own data.","Don't label your own product AI unless it is."],
close="AI on the label isn't AI in the product.",
q="Have you bought something \"AI-powered\" that wasn't?",
scene='"With natural extracts."\nOne drop in the whole bottle.',
hook="AI washing is the same trick: AI on the label, little in the product.",
extra="Ask what the AI actually does."),
]
